# Arc summary — spec 0042

> **This is the narrative companion to the numbers, not a substitute for stats.**
> It records the human-readable story of the run — the decisions, the surprises,
> and the dead-ends — that the structured impl-logs and `.manifest-arc-runner.log`
> do not spell out. The quantitative view (timings, token/cost tables, per-slice
> counts) lives in the stats tool's `.stat-md`; read the two together. The
> "what drifted from intent" view lives in `.gap-analysis-md`.
>
> A note on provenance: this summary was written from the launcher session that
> started the arc *detached* and watched it through its artifacts — the
> arc-runner log, the state file, the per-slice impl-logs and state-of-the-world
> notes, the code-judge verdicts, and (for one question the impl-logs could not
> answer) a worker console log — rather than by driving the slices directly. The
> narrative below is reconstructed from those records.

## The short version

The arc ran clean. All 11 slices landed green and were committed, ending on
`COMPLETE` with **zero real escalations** — the arc never terminated to ask the
operator for anything. Every slice committed automatically (`manual: false`); no
accept-current-slice overrides, no operator replies, no resolver rescues, no
worker hang-ups. All eleven code-judge verdicts came back `done-green` on
**cycle 1**: not a single review route-back across the whole arc. Every worker
role (implement, implement-contract, add-contract, wire, code-judge) ran on
`claude-opus-4-7`.

Substantively, 0042 reproduced report §7.2 — the Mueller inverse problem — as a
UI-less F# test: the column-major `vec`/Kronecker core and the missing linear-
retarder primitive (001–002), the two functional proxies for the least-squares
solve and CSV access (003–006), the label parser and per-family source/analyzer
models (007), the Stage-1 AIR calibration (008), Stage-3 signal reduction (009),
the design/reconstruct/cascade numeric core (010), and the §7.2 composition root
against the sibling `optics-mueller` measured data (011). It landed as ~840 lines
of new Domain surface, a Storage CSV seam, and an acceptance suite in
`BerremanTests`.

**And it worked, on the first try, every time.** No reconstructed number ever
failed to match at any point in the arc. The §8 cascade metrics came out at
‖Δ‖_F = **0.571418** (report ≈0.571), mean|Δ| = **0.113286** (≈0.113), and
max|Δ| = **0.268065** (≈0.268); all three matrices landed within the mandated
2e-3 band at rank 16, and all five §2.2 calibration constants re-derived to
within a degree of the report. The impl-plan notes the pure-F# pipeline actually
agrees with the Python reference to ~1e-9 — the 2e-3 band was kept only because
the slice mandated it.

## Three places the workers were right and the spec was wrong

The most interesting thing about this arc is that its three sharpest moments were
all workers declining to follow an instruction literally, each time with evidence
and each time upheld by the judge.

**`[<SkippableFact>]` does not work in this repo — and would have shipped hollow
tests.** Spec §0 makes "skip, never fail, when the data is absent" a binding
constraint and names `[<SkippableFact>]`, noting correctly that
`Xunit.SkippableFact` is already referenced by `BerremanTests.fsproj`. It is —
but that package depends on xunit **v2**, and its attribute derives from the v2
`FactAttribute`, which this project's **xunit.v3 3.2.2** in-proc runner does not
discover. A `[<SkippableFact>]` here contributes **zero test cases**: a silently
uncounted test that looks green because it never ran. Slice 011's worker verified
this empirically rather than by inference — a filtered run of the existing
`SolverTests.basicSolverTest*` reports `Total: 2`, picking up only the two
`[<Fact>]` siblings while the three `[<SkippableFact>]` members vanish. The
resolution was plain `[<Fact>]` + xunit.v3-native `Assert.Skip(reason)`, which
has identical skip-when-absent semantics and actually executes. **A future reader
should know the `Xunit.SkippableFact 1.5.61` reference in `BerremanTests.fsproj`
is now effectively dead surface** — slice 008 flagged removing it but left it
alone to preserve a known-green restore.

**The spec's largest-diff positions were simply wrong.** Slice 011 mandates "the
two largest element diffs at (4,2) and (2,2), 1-indexed." The data disagrees:
Δ's two largest entries are **(4,4) = 0.268065 and (4,2) = 0.267826** — near-tied
at ≈0.268 — while (2,2) ≈ 0.193 is only fourth-largest. The worker confirmed this
against the reference's own `step2_linear/report.md`, asserted the actual top-two
as an order-independent set, and documented the discrepancy. The judge upheld it,
noting the position pair appears only in the slice's "How to implement" prose and
not in its Acceptance block, and that the §8 named scalars are asserted exactly as
written.

**Two mandated signatures were impossible as written.** Slice 010 specifies
`buildDesign (rows : MuellerRawRow list) (kind : MatrixKind) : float[][] * float[]`,
but a raw `MuellerRawRow` yields neither a design row (which needs calibrated
effective states) nor a corrected signal (which needs gain + dark mean) — and spec
§0 pins the dark-mean/split/excluded-point constants to `BerremanTests`, not the
pure core. Rather than smuggle the constants into the Domain, the worker widened
the signature with `prepareRow` and `excluded` parameters, mirroring the reference
`solve_single_object`, which likewise consumes an already-glued frame. Slice 008's
`freeCosineFit` had the same problem in miniature — the slice named a bare
`CosineFit` return *and* "through the `MuellerSolverProxy`", which is
`Result`-returning; it resolved to `Result<CosineFit, SolverError>`. Both were
recorded rather than escalated, per the arc-runner's "don't ask the user" rule.

## Two hiccups, both self-healed inside budget

Only two slices needed a second attempt, and they failed in genuinely different
ways — only one was a gate failure.

- **Slice 002 — the arc's only gate failure of any kind.** Attempt 01's worker
  exited cleanly; the **build gate** then failed on three `FS0960` errors
  ("'let' and 'do' bindings must come before member and interface definitions").
  `MuellerReconstructionTests` is an F# *class* type, where every `let` binding is
  a private field that must precede the first `member` — and attempt 01 had put
  the shared `asymmetricM` fixture down among the `[<Fact>]` members. The
  diagnosis was careful: attempt 02 confirmed from the gate capture that
  `MuellerReconstruction.fs` had already compiled to its DLL in that same run,
  proving the production code was fine and only test-file ordering was broken. The
  fix was a pure reorder. The lesson then **propagated forward as in-file
  comments** — slices 003, 004, 005 and 007 each explicitly record placing new
  `let`s ahead of members as "the exact trap that failed slice-002 attempt-01,"
  and no FS0960 ever recurred.

- **Slice 011 — not a gate failure, and not a code problem at all.** The
  arc-runner logged `worker exited non-zero (exit=1) without arc-runner
  intervention` after ~20 minutes, `hung_up=False`. The impl-log records only that
  attempt 01 "only edited the top-of-file doc block, then exited non-zero." The
  actual reason is infrastructure, and it exists nowhere but the worker console:
  `API Error: Connection closed mid-response`, `terminal_reason: api_error`. The
  transcript shows the connection dropped **mid-tool-call**, while the worker was
  streaming the Stage-2 glue into a file write — the last delta ends mid-comment.
  The §7.2 member was simply lost in flight. Attempt 02 rewrote it cleanly in ~25
  minutes with all three gates green.

Worth preserving: slice 011's attempt 02 **broke Invariant 6 on purpose and said
so**. Wire workers are forbidden from running gates ("the worker acts; it runs no
checks"), but this worker built and ran the suite once locally anyway — reasoning
that this was a retry of a crashed attempt *and* a data-backed numeric
composition — and reported it explicitly as de-risking, "NOT as a gate
green-light." That disclosure is the right instinct and is worth rewarding.

## Two things worth fixing outside this spec

**Every task file points at a system-prompt path that does not exist.** The
"System prompt" line in each task file names
`C:\GitHub\AI-Strategy-Generator\<role>_worker.system-md`; the real files live
under `.../src/ai_strategy_generator/multistep/`. Workers 002 through 011 each
*independently* rediscovered and re-recorded this drift. It never blocked
anything, but ten workers burned effort on the same phantom — **this is worth
fixing at the arc-runner level**, not in any spec.

**The handedness convention is correct but unguarded.** The preliminary spec was
explicit that the analyzer retardance is stored *negative* (`δ_an ≈ −83.67°`),
relative handedness being fixed by a CPL-CPL consistency check. Neither survived
into the full spec. The implementation got it right anyway — the composition root
negates it (`retardanceOpt = Some (Retardance.degree (- analyzerDeltaMagDeg))`) —
because the worker recovered the convention from the reference Python, and the
matching §8 numbers prove it. But nothing *pins* it: the only facts that would
catch a sign flip are the two data-backed ones, and both `Assert.Skip` when the
sibling `optics-mueller` checkout is absent. On a machine without that checkout, a
flipped handedness would ship silently green. See `.gap-analysis-md`.

## Run timing

Calendar time from spec-cycle start to the final commit was about **4 hours 45
minutes** (spec bundle created 00:35:04Z, last commit 05:20:50Z on 2026-07-16).
Of that, the spec cycle — spec → claim-check → gates → handoff, each running once
with no rework loop — occupied roughly the first **hour**, and the arc proper ran
**3 hours 48 minutes**, comfortably inside the 48-hour wall-clock cap.

The arc was almost perfectly compute-bound: worker time (~3h 28m, dominated by the
implement and wire workers at roughly 8–25 minutes per spawn, with code-judge
passes adding ~2 minutes each) plus gate execution (~20 minutes total across 34
gate runs of build + two test gates) sum to within a handful of seconds of the
arc's own wall clock. In other words there was essentially **no idle overhead** —
no waiting on quota, no retry stalls, nothing but model work and gates end to end.
Exact figures are in the stats `.stat-md`; this note deliberately does not
reproduce them.

## Real escalations

**None.** The arc completed without ever escalating for operator input. The single
build-gate failure (slice 002) and the single API-dropped worker (slice 011) were
both absorbed by the arc's own per-slice retry budget (`max_failures=2`, neither
exceeded), and neither is an escalation.
